<%#
====================================================================
FILE: src/views/users.ejs
COURSE: CSE 340 - Web Backend Development (Week 5 Assignment)
====================================================================

PLAIN ENGLISH: The admin-only page that lists every registered user with
               their name, email, and role, in a table.
LOGIC: Rendered by showUsersPage in src/controllers/users.js, which sends
       title and users (the list from getAllUsers in src/models/users.js).
       The page only loads if requireRole('admin') on the /users route
       lets the request through.
WHY WE NEED IT: The W05 Assignment asks for a page where admins can see
                everyone registered in the system and their role.
LEARNING GAP: The data comes from the DATABASE (users table joined to the
              roles table), not the session. This page never checks the
              role by itself. The route lock does that before this page is
              ever rendered. No password hashes are here, because the
              model's SELECT never asks for them.
%>

<%# PLAIN ENGLISH: Add the shared top of the page (site header and menu).
    LOGIC: Pulls in src/views/partials/header.ejs, which also shows flash messages.
    WHY WE NEED IT: Every page looks the same at the top without copying code.
    LEARNING GAP: The dash in the opening tag prints the partial's HTML as real
                  HTML. An equals sign would show the tags as plain text. %>
<%- include('partials/header') %>

<main>
    <%# PLAIN ENGLISH: The page title at the top.
        LOGIC: title comes from showUsersPage (title: 'Registered Users').
        WHY WE NEED IT: Matches how every other page shows its title.
        LEARNING GAP: The equals sign tag prints text safely. %>
    <h1><%= title %></h1>

    <%# PLAIN ENGLISH: If there are users, show them in a table. If not, show a message.
        LOGIC: users is an array (a list). users.length is how many are in it.
               More than 0 = build the table. 0 = show "No users found."
        WHY WE NEED IT: A blank table with only headings would look broken.
        LEARNING GAP: There will always be at least one user here (the admin
                      looking at the page), but checking is still good practice. %>
    <% if (users.length > 0) { %>
        <p>All accounts registered in the system:</p>

        <%# PLAIN ENGLISH: A box around the table that lets it scroll sideways on small screens.
            LOGIC: The users-table-wrapper class in main.css adds overflow-x: auto.
            WHY WE NEED IT: Long email addresses could push the table off a phone screen.
            LEARNING GAP: The wrapper scrolls, not the whole page, so the layout stays tidy. %>
        <div class="users-table-wrapper">
            <table class="users-table">
                <%# PLAIN ENGLISH: The column headings.
                    LOGIC: thead holds the header row; th cells are headings.
                    WHY WE NEED IT: Tells the reader what each column means.
                    LEARNING GAP: scope="col" tells screen readers that each heading
                                  belongs to the column below it (accessibility). %>
                <thead>
                    <tr>
                        <th scope="col">Name</th>
                        <th scope="col">Email</th>
                        <th scope="col">Role</th>
                    </tr>
                </thead>

                <%# PLAIN ENGLISH: One row for each user in the list.
                    LOGIC: users.forEach runs once per user. Each time, "listedUser" is
                           one row from the database: { name, email, role_name }.
                    WHY WE NEED IT: Builds the table automatically, no matter how
                                    many people have registered.
                    LEARNING GAP: Inside the loop we call it "listedUser", not "user".
                                  "user" already means the LOGGED-IN person (from
                                  res.locals.user in server.js, used by the header).
                                  Reusing the same name could mix the two up.
                                  The names after the dot (name, email, role_name)
                                  must match the model's SELECT column names exactly. %>
                <tbody>
                    <% users.forEach(listedUser => { %>
                        <tr>
                            <td><%= listedUser.name %></td>
                            <td><%= listedUser.email %></td>
                            <td><%= listedUser.role_name %></td>
                        </tr>
                    <% }); %>
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </div>
    <% } else { %>
        <p>No users found.</p>
    <% } %>

    <%# PLAIN ENGLISH: A link back to the dashboard.
        LOGIC: A plain anchor tag pointing at /dashboard.
        WHY WE NEED IT: Admins reach this page from the dashboard, so this gives
                        them an easy way back.
        LEARNING GAP: Only admins can see this page at all, so this link doesn't
                      need its own role check. %>
    <p><a href="/dashboard">Back to Dashboard</a></p>
</main>

<%# PLAIN ENGLISH: Add the shared bottom of the page (site footer).
    LOGIC: Pulls in src/views/partials/footer.ejs.
    WHY WE NEED IT: Closes the page the same way as every other page.
    LEARNING GAP: The header partial opens tags that the footer partial closes,
                  so both are needed for a complete page. %>
<%- include('partials/footer') %>
